\subsection{\code{BaseRCorrelogramLayerProcessor$extract_main_title()}}{
  Title the chart the way the drawing does.

\code{plot.acf()} writes "Series v" above an \code{acf} and "v & w" above a
\code{ccf}, and the replayed object carries those as \verb{$series} and
\verb{$snames} -- but not usefully. Both are \code{deparse(substitute(x))}, and
the replay hands \code{stats} the recorded \emph{values} rather than the name
the caller wrote, so measured they come back as the whole series
pasted in: \verb{Series c(-2.0715334064552, -0.117989125730012, ...)}.
That is worse than no title at all.

The name is in the recorded call expression instead, which is kept as
the source text of the call -- measured, \code{"acf(v, lag.max = 3)"}. It
is used only when the argument is a bare symbol: a caller who wrote
\code{acf(rnorm(60))} named nothing, and titling the chart with the
expression that produced it would announce a call rather than a
series.

A caller's own \verb{main =} wins, which is what the inherited reading
already answers.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{BaseRCorrelogramLayerProcessor$extract_main_title(layer_info)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{layer_info}}{Information about the recorded plot call}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    The title, or NULL when the caller named nothing
  }
}

